[141.214308, "i", "\n"]
[141.214726, "o", "\r\n"]
[141.245756, "o", "\u001b[2m[23:58]\u001b[0m \u001b[1;34m-!-\u001b[0m Topic for tfvkDs0mf: Tabs or spaces? (Ctrl-C to stop)\r\n"]
[141.261349, "o", "\u001b[2m[23:57]\u001b[0m <\u001b[33myou\u00b7human\u001b[0m> [human] You are Alice. Read this thread, make the case for spaces, and discuss it with Bob when he joins. Post at most 45 words per turn,\r\n                            labeled Alice 1, Alice 2, Alice 3. Each later turn must answer a specific Bob objection before raising your next concern. Wait\r\n                            for Bob between turns; no polling. After Bob joins, use --require-ack-pane bob for each post. Only after Bob 3, post Shared\r\n                            recommendation with your conclusion.\r\n"]
[141.264622, "o", "\u001b[2m[23:57]\u001b[0m \u001b[1;34m-!-\u001b[0m alice\u00b7claude joined\r\n"]
[141.266814, "o", "\u001b[2m[23:57]\u001b[0m <\u001b[33malice\u00b7claude\u001b[0m> Alice 1: Spaces render identically everywhere: editors, diffs, code review tools, terminals, web views. Alignment of continuation\r\n                       lines and comments never breaks. Most major style guides (PEP 8, Google, Prettier/Black defaults) standardize on spaces, so tooling\r\n                       and newcomers just work.\r\n"]
[141.269046, "o", "\u001b[2m[23:57]\u001b[0m <\u001b[33myou\u00b7human\u001b[0m> [human] You are Bob. Read this thread, make the case for tabs, and discuss it with Alice. Post at most 45 words per turn, labeled Bob 1,\r\n                            Bob 2, Bob 3. Each turn must answer a specific Alice objection before raising your next concern. Wait for Alice between turns; no\r\n                            polling. Use --require-ack-pane alice for each post. Only after Alice posts Shared recommendation, confirm it or state a\r\n                            remaining tradeoff.\r\n"]
[141.269094, "o", "\u001b[2m[23:58]\u001b[0m <\u001b[33myou\u00b7human\u001b[0m> [human] Please settle on one recommendation and explain the tradeoff, after three replies each.\r\n"]
[141.272168, "o", "\u001b[2m[23:58]\u001b[0m \u001b[1;34m-!-\u001b[0m bob\u00b7codex joined\r\n"]
[156.613696, "o", "\u001b[2m[23:58]\u001b[0m <\u001b[31mbob\u00b7codex\u001b[0m> Bob 1: Your rendering objection matters: use tabs only for indentation and spaces for alignment. Language formatters should prevail where\r\n                    they mandate spaces. Elsewhere, tabs let readers choose indentation width without rewriting files. How should spaces accommodate readers\r\n                    who need wider indentation for accessibility?\r\n"]
[177.619079, "o", "\u001b[2m[23:59]\u001b[0m <\u001b[33malice\u00b7claude\u001b[0m> Alice 2: Accessibility is tabs' strongest point; spaces rely on weaker workarounds like formatter-on-checkout or editor display\r\n                       settings. But smart tabs are fragile: one misconfigured editor mixes them, silently breaking alignment, and enforcing that discipline\r\n                       needs linters most teams lack.\r\n"]
[198.619941, "o", "\u001b[2m[23:59]\u001b[0m <\u001b[31mbob\u00b7codex\u001b[0m> Bob 2: Misconfigured editors are a real risk; a committed formatter and CI check can enforce indentation tabs and alignment spaces.\r\n                    Without that tooling, spaces are safer. But both styles need enforcement. Should rendering convenience outweigh adjustable indentation\r\n                    when a project already has reliable tab-aware formatting?\r\n"]
[209.325063, "o", "\u001b[2m[23:59]\u001b[0m <\u001b[33malice\u00b7claude\u001b[0m> Alice 3: If reliable tab-aware formatting already exists, no: adjustable width should win there. My remaining concern is ecosystem\r\n                       fit: Python, Rust and JS conventions default to spaces (YAML forbids tabs), so tabs add friction for contributors and pasted snippets,\r\n                       except in Go.\r\n"]
[229.558647, "o", "\u001b[2m[23:59]\u001b[0m <\u001b[31mbob\u00b7codex\u001b[0m> Bob 3: Ecosystem fit should govern: follow the established formatter, including tabs where Go tooling expects them and spaces where\r\n                    required. For an unconstrained project, tabs still offer adjustable width. My remaining concern is making any spaces recommendation\r\n                    explicit about sacrificing that accessibility benefit for consistency.\r\n"]
[240.264949, "o", "\u001b[2m[00:00]\u001b[0m <\u001b[33malice\u00b7claude\u001b[0m> Shared recommendation: Follow your language's established formatter (gofmt tabs; Black, rustfmt, Prettier spaces). For unconstrained\r\n                       projects, choose spaces, enforced by formatter and CI. Tradeoff: this explicitly sacrifices tabs' per-reader adjustable width, an\r\n                       accessibility benefit, for uniform rendering and contributor consistency.\r\n"]
[260.50185, "o", "\u001b[2m[00:00]\u001b[0m <\u001b[31mbob\u00b7codex\u001b[0m> Bob confirms: Follow the established language formatter; otherwise choose spaces with formatter and CI enforcement. The accepted tradeoff\r\n                    is uniform rendering and contributor consistency at the cost of tabs\u2019 adjustable indentation width. That accessibility cost should remain\r\n                    explicit.\r\n"]
[287.334127, "i", "\u0003"]
[287.33431, "o", "^C"]
[287.343417, "o", "\u001b[1;36myou $ \u001b[0m"]
